The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its primary responsibility for the maintenance of international peace and security,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to the principle of non-use of force in international relations except in accordance with the Charter,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018),
Reaffirming that any use of chemical weapons by anyone constitutes a violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,
Expressing grave concern over the reports of alleged chemical weapons use in Douma, Syrian Arab Republic, on 7 April 2018, and condemning any use of chemical weapons by any party,
Stressing that the investigation of all allegations of chemical weapons use must be independent, impartial, thorough, transparent and consistent with the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on Their Destruction and with the mandate of the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing high tensions in the region and at the risk of further escalation,
Emphasizing the need for all parties to exercise restraint and to avoid any actions that could further complicate the situation and undermine the prospects for peace,
Reaffirming the need for full implementation of resolution 2401 (2018), including the cessation of hostilities and safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access throughout Syria,
Reaffirming its support for a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
1. Condemns any use of chemical weapons by any party and expresses grave concern over the reports of alleged chemical weapons use in Douma on 7 April 2018; and demands that all parties refrain from the use of chemical weapons and comply with their obligations under international law;
2. Supports the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission in its investigation of the reports of chemical weapons use in Douma, and calls for full, safe and unhindered access by the Fact-Finding Mission to all relevant sites, and for the preservation of evidence;
3. Requests the OPCW Director-General to report to the Security Council on the findings of the Fact-Finding Mission as soon as possible and no later than 30 days from the date of adoption of this resolution, and requests the Secretary-General to keep the Council informed;
4. Reaffirms the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and calls upon all States to respect these principles;
5. Calls upon all parties and all Member States to exercise restraint and to avoid any actions that could further escalate tensions in the region or undermine the prospects for a political solution;
6. Demands that all parties fully comply with resolution 2401 (2018), including by ceasing hostilities and ensuring safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access;
7. Urges all parties to resume negotiations under the auspices of the United Nations without preconditions, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015), and reaffirms its commitment to a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process;
8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

The Security Council,
Recalling the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations and its primary responsibility for the maintenance of international peace and security,
Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to the principle of non-use of force in international relations except in accordance with the Charter,
Reaffirming the inherent right of self-defence in accordance with Article 51 of the Charter,
Recalling its resolutions 2118 (2013), 2209 (2015), 2235 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017) and 2401 (2018),
Reaffirming that any use of chemical weapons by anyone constitutes a violation of international law and a threat to international peace and security,
Condemning in the strongest terms the reported chemical weapons attack in Douma, Syrian Arab Republic, on 7 April 2018, and condemning any use of chemical weapons by any party,
Recalling that the Syrian authorities bear responsibility for ensuring that chemical weapons are not used in territory under their control, and stressing that all parties must comply with their obligations under international law,
Stressing that the investigation of all allegations of chemical weapons use must be independent, objective, impartial, thorough, transparent and consistent with the Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production, Stockpiling and Use of Chemical Weapons and on Their Destruction and with the mandate of the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission,
Expressing grave concern at the continuing high tensions in the region and at the risk of further escalation,
Emphasizing the need for all parties to exercise restraint and to avoid any actions that could further complicate the situation and undermine the prospects for peace,
Stressing that there can be no military solution to the Syrian conflict and that the crisis can be resolved only through an inclusive Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process under United Nations auspices,
Reaffirming the need for full implementation of resolution 2401 (2018), including the cessation of hostilities and safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access throughout Syria,
Reaffirming its support for a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015),
1. Condemns in the strongest terms the reported chemical weapons attack in Douma on 7 April 2018, stresses that those responsible for any use of chemical weapons must be held accountable, and demands that all parties refrain from the use of chemical weapons and comply with their obligations under international law;
2. Demands that all parties, in particular the Syrian authorities, cooperate fully with the OPCW Fact-Finding Mission and provide immediate, full, safe and unhindered access to all relevant sites and persons, and calls for the preservation of evidence;
3. Requests the OPCW Director-General to report to the Security Council on the findings of the Fact-Finding Mission as soon as possible and no later than 30 days from the date of adoption of this resolution, requests the Secretary-General to report to the Council on any impediments to the Fact-Finding Mission's access, and decides to consider the OPCW report before taking any further action;
4. Stresses that those responsible for any use of chemical weapons in the Syrian Arab Republic must be held accountable and that all parties must cooperate fully with the OPCW and its Fact-Finding Mission;
5. Requests the Secretary-General, in coordination with the OPCW Director-General, to present to the Council within 30 days options for an independent and impartial mechanism to investigate reports of chemical weapons use in Syria and to report on steps to ensure accountability, for the Council's consideration;
6. Reaffirms the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic and calls upon all States to respect these principles and to act in accordance with the Charter of the United Nations, including Article 2(4);
7. Calls upon all parties to the Syrian conflict to exercise restraint and avoid actions that could further escalate tensions in the region or undermine the prospects for a political solution, and calls upon all States to respect the Charter of the United Nations and international law;
8. Demands that all parties fully comply with resolution 2401 (2018), including by ceasing hostilities and ensuring safe, unimpeded and sustained humanitarian access throughout Syria, including across conflict lines and across borders, and to facilitate the medical evacuation of the sick and wounded;
9. Urges all parties to resume negotiations under the auspices of the United Nations without preconditions, in accordance with resolution 2254 (2015), and reaffirms its commitment to a Syrian-led and Syrian-owned political process, with the full, equal and meaningful participation of women in all stages of the political process;
10. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

